course: dictionary
lesson: 8835
title: Shiny: bright, smooth and new-looking
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Shiny is an adjective, and its forms are shinier and shiniest. It means smooth and bright, so light bounces off it and it seems to glow. «A shiny glaze, which is a thin coat of glass, makes a mug waterproof.» The glaze is smooth and bright, and that is what shiny describes here.
explain: Shiny can also mean new and clean-looking. «Fun does not depend on how new or shiny a game is.» The clue is the word new beside it: shiny describes how a game looks, and the sentence says looks do not make a game fun.
explain: Scientists use shiny to sort materials. Shiny materials like metals reflect light well, and dull materials do not. «A flask has two shiny walls that bounce heat rays back» shows that a shiny surface reflects, so the heat stays inside.
explain: Shiny tells you how something looks, not whether it is safe. «A boy in Scotland ate shiny red berries from a bush in the park; he was sick and dizzy» is a warning. Always ask an adult before you eat anything you find outdoors.
fact: Shiny means smooth and bright, so it reflects light and seems to shine.
fact: Shiny can also mean new and clean-looking, as in «a shiny new bike».
fact: The forms of shiny are shiny, shinier and shiniest.
fact: In science, metals are usually shiny, and shiny materials reflect light well.
fact: Shiny does not mean safe: the berries in the Scottish story looked shiny and still made the boy ill.
exercise: In «a shiny new bike», shiny can mean what? | Very quiet | *New and clean-looking | Old and broken | Heavy and cold
exercise: Why do scientists say metals are shiny? | Because metals always float | Because metals are soft | Because metals are always red | *Because shiny metals reflect light well
exercise: «He ate shiny red berries and was sick.» What lesson does this story teach? | Red berries are always good to eat | *Shiny does not mean safe to eat | Shiny berries are always sweet | Parks have no food at all
test: Shiny is an adjective. Which form means the most shiny? | shinier | shiny | shinest | *shiniest
test: «Long ago, small shiny cowrie shells were used as money.» In this sentence, shiny describes the shells as what? | Very large and heavy | Old and broken | Wet and cold | *Smooth and bright
exam: «Fun does not depend on how new or shiny a game is.» What does this sentence tell you? | Shiny games are always more fun | New games are never fun | *Shiny can mean new-looking, and fun is about more than looks | Games are only fun when they are shiny
